# Cursor rules — <Your Project Name>
#
# This is a template. Copy it to your project root as `.cursorrules`. It is authored in
# drunken-guild's `templates/.cursorrules`; a project's own copy is its own to change.
#
# It is deliberately thin. The rules live in ONE file, the project's `AGENTS.md`, and this one
# points at it. A rulebook per tool is a rulebook per tool that drifts: the set this replaced told
# Cursor to park work on a board that no longer existed, three weeks after the board was retired.

## Read `AGENTS.md` first. It is the authority here.

Before any work, read `AGENTS.md` at the project root. It is vendor-neutral and binds every agent
in the project — the Jira lifecycle, the approval protocol, git, what must never be deleted, the
build and test commands. `CLAUDE.md` here is only a one-line pointer at it (REQ-015). Where this
file and `AGENTS.md` disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins.

`AGENTS.md` also says where this project's documents are — brief, requirements, spec,
architecture, policy. Read the ones that exist; do not invent the ones that do not.

## What is different for Cursor

- **MCP.** Connect `drunken-jira-mcp` under Settings → MCP, as a `command` with no arguments —
  every tool takes the project id as its first argument instead. It is the only coordination path:
  no shell script, no local board, no ticket list kept in a file.
- **Approvals.** If the Boss is reading the conversation, ask there. Otherwise send one
  notification and park the task — do not perform the action, take the next unblocked one, and
  pick the answer up when a task finishes or a session starts, never mid-task.
- **Commits.** Cursor does not commit on its own. When you do, follow the commit format in
  `AGENTS.md`, not a format remembered from another project.
